  "text": "**Assessment of the three answers**\n\n- **Solution 2 (answer 3)** claims a degree-4 counterexample exists. That conflicts with the published degree-4 result (Braun and Oréfice-Okamoto, 2016), which I believe shows that deg p ≤ 4 forces injectivity. Its cubic-case argument is also hard to check, so I give it little weight.\n- **Solution 3 (answer 4)** claims a degree-5 counterexample exists, attributed to Braun and Fernandes. I recall the degree-5 work by the same group as a positive result, so this attribution looks doubtful. If the degree-5 injectivity result holds, Solution 3 is wrong.\n- **Solution 1 (answer 5)** is consistent with the literature sequence I recall (deg ≤ 2, 3, 4, and then 5 proven) and with the question's implication that the bound is sharp. Its specific claim of a degree-6 counterexample is unverified, and I cannot check it here, so this is a judgment call rather than a confirmed result.\n\nI could not verify any of the cited papers, since I have no search tools in this session. Solution 1 is the most plausible of the three, with moderate confidence.\n\nCHOICE: 1",
